    "created_at": "2026-06-09T22:58:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-09T22:58:54Z",
    "body": "Some time ago I had to deal with a situation where a _cookie consent_ dialog was sometimes obscured by another dialog, and to find a way to guard against this situation.\nSo I think there are perfectly reasonable production use cases for why application code would want to know when new content enters the top layer; I endorse the addition of a top layer API.\n\nBut how much should such an API expose?\nWhat could potentially serve as a kind of guiding light is knowing the boundary of what is currently possible in userland;\nMy reasoning being: if such an API would be more restrictive than what's already possible in userland then adoption of the API would seriously suffer.\n\n## What's currently already possible\n\nAs others have pointed out, observing a handful of DOM events and thereby keeping track of any open modal `<dialog>`, `[popover]`, etc. already gives us a good idea of what's in the top layer and in what order. As far as dialogs/popovers that reside in shadow DOM are concerned: we can discover those as long as it is open shadow DOM (by listening for composed `focus` events when a dialog/popover in shadow DOM opens and then `querySelectorAll` on the shadowRoot). With closed shadow DOM we are out of luck.\n\nThus we can create a utility that offers\n- at any point in time reading the current top layer stack\n  with each stack entry offering:\n  - a reference to the corresponding DOM element\n  - information about the nature of the entry (modal dialog, popover, fullscreen, or customized select picker)\n- registering a callback that is invoked when entries enter or leave the top layer\n\nChoosing the _Observer_ pattern that is already established by `MutationObserver`, `ResizeObserver`, `IntersectionObserver` etc. I implemented the above feature set as [top-layer-observer](https://github.com/teetotum/top-layer-observer)\n\n## What a native API would need to offer\n\nTherefore in my opinion a native API should offer...\n1. the capabilities listed in the above section,\n  for top layer entries from the regular DOM\n  and for top layer entries from open shadow DOM\n2. additionally: the inclusion of top layer entries (i.e. triggering the callback and being represented as an entry in the top layer stack data structure) from closed shadow DOM, albeit \"anonymised\" to prevent leaking internals.\n  That means \"reference to the corresponding DOM element\" will only provide a reference to the host element, and \"information about the nature of the entry\" will be `\"unknown\"` (instead of `\"popover\"` etc.)",
